<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the NEPacketTunnelProvider.

  Authority: ADR-0016 §11.2's hardening table's iOS/iPadOS row, verbatim:

    com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension = [packet-tunnel-provider]
    com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api          = [allow-vpn]
    shared keychain-access-groups and App Group for app+extension
    extension memory budgeted to the 15 MB floor

  ADR-0020 §11.3's iOS row explains why the two SHARED entitlements are shared
  and not scoped tighter: `kSecAttrAccessGroup` is "the shared keychain access
  group from the App ID; the vault file lives in the App Group container", and
  the app and the extension are two processes of one app identity.

  That sharing is a DECLARED RESIDUAL, not an oversight. ADR-0016 §11.4's
  compromised-process table, iOS column: "use DeviceKey as signing oracle = Yes
  — declared residual", because no privilege boundary on this platform can scope
  below app identity. PS-24 condition 4 says the same from the other end: PS-1's
  "sole holder of the key handle" clause is "on HC-2 only, a declared residual
  rather than an enforceable property, tested as a declaration in P16."

  STATUS: written, not compiled and not signed.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
    <array>
        <string>packet-tunnel-provider</string>
    </array>

    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api</key>
    <array>
        <string>allow-vpn</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Shared with the app. ADR-0020 §11.3's iOS row and ST-22's co-location:
         the identity key, the SEK and the S-53 anchor all live in ONE access
         group, under ONE accessibility class. -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)group.net.twinvpn.client</string>
    </array>

    <!-- ADR-0020 §11.9's iOS row: `store_root` is the App Group container,
         opened by THE PROVIDER ONLY (ST-30's single opener). The app has the
         same entitlement because it is one app identity, and refrains by
         discipline rather than by sandbox — which is exactly why ST-30 exists as
         a rule and PS-24 condition 2 assigns a writer per fact. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>group.net.twinvpn.client</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
